record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-2b-large-transform-penalty-screen/1 : work {
    title "Phase 6.2b: screen Phase 6.2 Result B alone -- the per-transform distortion scale, without the CSF curve"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-cli/src/main.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/lib.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/request.rs",
        path "test-set/**"
    ]
    intent """
        Phase 6.3 pairs two independent corrections: a first-principles CSF frequency weight (Result A) and a per-transform level correction (Result B). Result B is far cheaper to test -- one scalar per transform size, no CSF derivation, no per-cell table -- and it answers the prior question 6.3 depends on: does re-pricing the cover objective move Butteraugli at all? Split it out and screen it alone, so a null result costs a day rather than a week and a positive result de-risks 6.3.

        Add CoverSizePenalty as a research control on EncodeRequest, defaulting to Neutral. block_cost_bounded already brings coefficient error into the sample domain by multiplying by side^2; Measured multiplies that by a per-transform constant so a large candidate is charged what Phase 6.2 measured it to cost. Neutral returns exactly 1.0, so IEEE multiplication by one leaves the shipped objective bit-identical rather than merely close.

        The constants convert Result B's butteraugli excess into an energy-equivalent distortion ratio. Butteraugli is not linear in injected energy: over the measured 4x energy step it follows ba ~ E^p with p = 0.4477, 0.4477 and 0.4433 for the three sizes (mean 0.4462), so a butteraugli ratio r means r^(1/p) of distortion. That maps the measured 3.8% and 5.7% mean excesses onto multipliers 1.0000 / 1.0881 / 1.1331 for DCT8x8 / DCT16x16 / DCT32x32, stable across both probe amplitudes.

        Screen neutral against measured at exactly matched rate: encode neutral at the requested bpp, take its achieved byte count as the cap, and encode measured to that exact cap. Decode both with the pinned djxl and score with the in-repo SSIMULACRA2 and butteraugli. Test-set inputs still lack provenance sidecars, so the table is diagnostic rather than a promoted corpus baseline -- the same status Phase 5O recorded.

        Honest-negative acceptable and pre-registered: if a correctly-priced transform scale does not move Butteraugli, that localises the deficit away from cover selection and is the more valuable of the two outcomes for 6.3's scoping. Production stays Neutral unless the direction is uniformly non-regressing.
        """
    acceptance {
        check neutral-byte-identical {
            statement """
                Neutral's multiplier is exactly 1.0 for every transform size and an explicitly-neutral request encodes byte-identically to the default request, so the shipped objective is unchanged.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-2b-neutral-byte-identical-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check penalty-moves-the-cover {
            statement """
                Tests prove the measured multipliers are strictly increasing in transform size and that, on a fixture which merges under the neutral objective, charging large transforms more reduces merging rather than increasing it.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-2b-penalty-moves-cover-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check quality-screen {
            statement """
                Matched-rate Butteraugli and SSIMULACRA2 across the screened scenes and both rates decide whether the per-transform scale is carried into Phase 6.3 as a promoted correction, or recorded as an honest negative that localises the deficit away from cover selection.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-2b-quality-negative-2026-08-12/2 ]
        }
    }
    blocks [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-3-frequency-weight-cover-objective ]
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-2-per-transform-step-shapes ]
    derived_from [ @jpegxl-rs.observation.one-frequency-curve-fits-all-squares-2026-08-12 ]
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.optimization-correctness-gates
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-3-frequency-weight-cover-objective/1 : work {
    title "Phase 6.3: a first-principles frequency weight in the cover/CfL objective"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-cli/src/main.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/lib.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/quantize.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/request.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/tests/rate_loop.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/tests/vardct_oracle.rs",
        path "test-set/**"
    ]
    intent """
        Replaces the superseded quantizer-normalised plan, which Phase 6.2 falsified: weighting by 1/step^2 is approximately right at DCT8x8 (2.86x step ratio) and badly wrong at DCT32x32 (13.05x), where it would price high-frequency error as cheap exactly where measurement says it is most expensive.

        NARROWED BY PHASE 6.2b. The original plan paired a frequency weight (6.2 Result A) with a per-transform level correction (Result B). 6.2b screened Result B alone and it is an honest negative: butteraugli regressed in 9 of 14 matched-rate cells (mean +2.03%) and SSIMULACRA2 in 11 of 14, because charging large transforms more makes the cover split more and the DctSelect and per-varblock bits those splits cost exceed what the better size choice buys. Result B is therefore NOT part of this change. The CoverSizePenalty control stays in place, production-neutral and bit-identical, so the combination can still be tested cheaply.

        So: give block_cost_bounded's Y-channel distortion term one per-cell multiplier, a contrast-sensitivity weight evaluated at each cell's normalised spatial frequency on that transform's own grid. Phase 6.2 Result A licenses a single curve for all three squares -- the measured curves agree to 1.15x once levelled, six of nine shared bins within 5%. Derive the CSF independently and *check* it against that measured curve, never fit it: that is what keeps the perceptual model this project's own (AGENTS.md section 2, and the butteraugli note in the workspace manifest). Pre-register the agreement tolerance before looking at the fit.

        Behind a research feature flag, with the flat objective as the byte-identical default until a corpus gate passes. X and B stay flat; chroma is Phase 6.4.

        Required work beyond the multiplier: recalibrate HfQuantizers::lambda, whose derivation (16 / mean(s^2) over the non-LLF DCT8x8 cells) assumes an unweighted sample-domain distortion term -- a stale lambda would silently reweight rate against distortion and confound the screen. Prove the flag off is byte-identical, prove the weighted scorer agrees with a direct recomputation on fixtures, then screen at matched rate under Contract B.

        PRIOR, STATED BEFORE RUNNING. 6.2b repriced one axis of this same objective correctly and the perceptual result did not follow. The frequency axis is a much larger mispricing (2.65x against 1.09-1.13x) and applies within every candidate rather than only between sizes, so this is not the same experiment. But a second null would strongly suggest the deficit is not in cover/CfL selection at all -- HfQuantizer::choose is still nearest-reconstruction rather than RD, so those two decisions are all any of this can move -- and that the next round belongs on the quantizer. That outcome is plan-sanctioned and is the main reason to run this before anything larger.
        """
    acceptance {
        check csf-checked-not-fitted {
            statement """
                The contrast-sensitivity curve is derived independently of the measurement and its agreement with Phase 6.2 Result A's measured curve is reported against a tolerance stated before the comparison, so the weight is checked rather than fitted.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-3-csf-agreement-fail-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check default-byte-identical {
            statement """
                With the flag off, the corpus fingerprints are byte-identical to the pre-6.3 encoder for the same requests, so the flat objective remains shipped behaviour until a gate passes.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-3-flat-byte-identical-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check quality-screen {
            statement """
                Matched-rate Butteraugli and SSIMULACRA2 across the screened scenes and both rates decide whether the re-priced objective is promoted under Contract B, or recorded as an honest negative that localises the deficit away from cover and CfL selection and redirects the track to the quantizer.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-3-quality-screen-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check scorer-proof {
            statement """
                Tests prove the weighted term matches a direct recomputation from the same reconstruction error, that the weight is evaluated on each transform's own normalised frequency grid, that it applies to Y alone, and that lambda is recalibrated into the new units rather than carried over.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-3-scorer-recomputation-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
    }
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-2b-large-transform-penalty-screen ]
    derived_from [
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.one-frequency-curve-fits-all-squares-2026-08-12,
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.transform-size-axis-is-not-the-deficit-2026-08-12
    ]
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.optimization-correctness-gates
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
    supersedes [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-3-quantizer-normalised-y-distortion/1 ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-3-quantizer-normalised-y-distortion/1 : work {
    title "Phase 6.3: land quantizer-normalised Y distortion in the cover/CfL objective, behind a flag"
    state superseded
    scope [
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-cli/src/main.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/lib.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/quantize.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/request.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/tests/rate_loop.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/tests/vardct_oracle.rs",
        path "test-set/**"
    ]
    intent """
        Change block_cost_bounded's distortion term from a flat sum of squared dequantized-coefficient errors to a quantizer-normalised one -- sum (delta_k / step_k)^2 rather than sum delta_k^2 -- for the Y channel only, behind a research feature flag with the flat objective as the byte-identical default until a corpus gate passes.

        Phase 6.1 measured this as cutting Y frequency mispricing from 2.65x to 1.86x using nothing but the I.2.5 default matrices HfQuantizer already builds, at the cost of one precomputed per-cell multiply in the scoring loop and no change to the quantizer. The weight is first-principles rather than fitted to butteraugli, which keeps the perceptual model this project's own (AGENTS.md section 2).

        X and B stay flat. Phase 6.1's chroma numbers were taken at amplitudes far outside any real operating point and are not a verdict; Phase 6.4 is the chroma measurement.

        Blocked on Phase 6.2. The change alters how a large square transform is priced against four sub-quadrants, and a weight validated only for DCT8x8 would bias that comparison in a new direction. Do not land this before 6.2 reports whether the sizes share one frequency curve.

        Required work: recalibrate HfQuantizers::lambda, whose current derivation (16 / mean(s^2) over the non-LLF DCT8x8 cells) assumes the distortion term is in sample-domain squared error -- normalising by step changes those units, and a stale lambda would silently reweight rate against distortion and confound the screen. Prove the flag off is byte-identical, prove the normalised scorer agrees with a direct recomputation on fixtures, then screen the six-scene matrix at matched rate under the existing Contract B rules.

        Honest-negative acceptable and pre-registered as such: a better-priced ruler can still fail to move Butteraugli if the deficit does not live in cover and CfL selection -- HfQuantizer::choose remains nearest-reconstruction rather than RD, so those two decisions are all this can touch. That outcome would itself localise the deficit and is a plan-sanctioned result, not a failure to chase.
        """
    acceptance {
        check default-byte-identical {
            statement """
                With the flag off, the corpus fingerprints are byte-identical to the pre-6.3 encoder for the same requests, so the flat objective remains the shipped behaviour until a gate passes.
                """
            method command
        }
        check quality-screen {
            statement """
                Matched-rate Butteraugli and SSIMULACRA2 across the six-scene matrix decide whether the re-priced objective is promoted under Contract B, or recorded as an honest negative that localises the deficit away from cover and CfL selection.
                """
            method observation
        }
        check scorer-proof {
            statement """
                Tests prove the quantizer-normalised term matches a direct recomputation from the same reconstruction error and steps, that it applies to Y alone, and that lambda is recalibrated into the new units rather than carried over.
                """
            method command
        }
    }
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-2-per-transform-step-shapes ]
    derived_from [
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.quantizer-normalised-distortion-is-the-lever-2026-08-12
    ]
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.optimization-correctness-gates
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-4-chroma-controlled-operating-point/1 : work {
    title "Phase 6.4: measure chroma frequency response at a controlled operating point"
    state completed
    scope [ path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-conformance/tests/perceptual_frequency.rs" ]
    intent """
        Phase 6.1's X and B results (2.35x to 6.76x, 1.56x to 11.60x) are not a verdict on chroma. The amplitude ladder scales one constant by the channel's standard deviation, which is sound for Phase 6.0's constant shape but not for a step shape spanning 8.23x (X) or 20.67x (B): the highest-frequency injections land far outside any operating point the encoder reaches, and butteraugli's max-norm aggregation saturates there. The measurement cannot currently distinguish a real chroma mispricing from metric saturation.

        Replace the fixed amplitude ladder with a per-cell amplitude search that holds the *realized* butteraugli distance inside a target band -- the 1 to 3 range the corpus actually operates in -- and report the amplitude each cell needed. At a controlled operating point the comparable quantity is no longer the raw score but the injected energy required to reach a fixed perceptual cost, which inverts cleanly into a weight and does not saturate by construction.

        Re-run both the equal-coefficient-error and step-proportional sweeps for X and B under that control, and re-run Y as well so the new methodology can be cross-checked against Y's already-stable result: if controlled-amplitude Y reproduces 6.0's 2.65x and 6.1's 1.86x, the method is validated and the chroma numbers can be believed.

        Until this completes, quantizer normalisation stays off for X and B (Phase 6.3 is Y-only).
        """
    acceptance {
        check chroma-decided {
            statement """
                X and B each get an explicit verdict on whether quantizer-normalised distortion should apply to them, or a stated reason why the question remains open.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-4-chroma-verdict-2026-08-13/1 ]
        }
        check controlled-amplitude {
            statement """
                Each cell's amplitude is chosen so the realized butteraugli distance falls inside a stated target band, and the amplitude required is reported alongside the score so the weight is derived from injected energy at fixed perceptual cost rather than from a score that may have saturated.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-4-controlled-harness-2026-08-13/1 ]
        }
        check method-cross-checked {
            statement """
                Y re-measured under the controlled-amplitude method is compared against Phase 6.0's 2.65x and Phase 6.1's 1.86x, and the record states whether the new method reproduces them and therefore whether the chroma numbers can be believed.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-4-y-crosscheck-2026-08-13/1 ]
        }
    }
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-1-quantizer-normalised-residual ]
    derived_from [
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.quantizer-normalised-distortion-is-the-lever-2026-08-12
    ]
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.optimization-correctness-gates
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-5-quant-donor-weight/1 : work {
    title "Phase 6.5: a frequency weight donated by the standard's own DCT8x8 matrix"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-cli/src/main.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/csf.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/lib.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/request.rs",
        path "test-set/**"
    ]
    intent """
        Phase 6.3 showed frequency weighting is the live lever in block_cost_bounded and that a Mannos-Sakrison detection CSF is the wrong curve. Replace the curve.

        OPTION 1 IS CLOSED, and closing it explains 6.3. The canonical DCT-domain per-coefficient models -- Ahumada-Peterson (1992), Peterson-Ahumada-Watson (1993), Watson's DCTune (1993), Nill (1985) -- are all detection-threshold models: band-pass with a low-frequency dip and a high-frequency rolloff HARDER than Mannos-Sakrison, not softer. AP92 predicts about 0.037 of peak sensitivity at Nyquist where the measurement says about 1.05, roughly 28x off against Mannos-Sakrison's 3.1x. Adopting them would be strictly worse than what 6.3 already rejected. Georgeson and Sullivan (1975) supply the reason: above threshold, perceived contrast becomes largely independent of spatial frequency (contrast constancy), so a detection CSF is the wrong regime for judging quantization error in real content, where low-frequency error is highly visible.

        OPTION 2, CORRECTED. Its original wording presumed the encoder's quantization geometry is a frequency model. It is not: the I.2.5 default step shapes disagree 3.34x across sizes in normalised frequency, against the measured perceptual curve's 1.15x -- the matrices are per-size tuned. The fix is to take ONE size's matrix as a donor curve in normalised frequency and resample it onto every transform's own grid. Scored against the target that gives median 1.41x, against the CSF's 2.06x and a 1.18x ceiling for a curve fitted directly to butteraugli. DCT8x8 is the donor: it wins outright over DCT16x16 (1.90x), DCT32x32 (2.62x) and the geometric mean of all three (2.34x), and it is the operating point HfQuantizers::lambda is calibrated at.

        Option 3 is not taken: the remaining 1.41x to 1.18x gap does not justify making the perceptual model libjxl-derived.

        Implementation reuses the Phase 6.3 plumbing unchanged -- one new weight constructor in csf.rs, one variant on CoverFrequencyWeight, one match arm, one CLI value. Flat stays the byte-identical default.

        TWO GATING CORRECTIONS carried from planning. Phase 6.3's 1.35x worst-bin tolerance was unachievable by its entire model class: the best smooth two-parameter shape FITTED to the target cannot beat 1.72x worst-bin, so that gate was never a fair test. And worst-bin is dominated by the two artifact bins (f below 0.1 and above 0.9), so median is the primary statistic here and worst-interior the secondary.

        HONEST EXPECTATION, pre-registered. Over f > 0.6 on DCT8x8 the CSF charges 0.41x what the target says and the donor charges 0.65x, so the donor removes roughly 40% of what caused 6.3's 2 bpp collapse, not all of it. Expect the high-rate arm to improve markedly without necessarily clearing Contract B. The falsifiable claim is that the 2 bpp arm improves markedly on 6.3's +13.10% mean while 1 bpp retains most of its 7/7 SSIMULACRA2 win; if 2 bpp does not improve, the rate split is not the curve's doing and the next round belongs on the quantizer, which is still nearest-reconstruction rather than RD.
        """
    acceptance {
        check agreement-reproduced {
            statement """
                The implemented donor reproduces the planning analysis against the measured target: median ratio at or below 1.60x pooled over all three sizes and worst-interior at or below 2.20x. This gate catches an implementation that fails to reproduce the analysis; it cannot claim predictive success, because the donor already scored 1.41x/2.02x on archived data before any code was written.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-5-agreement-pass-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check default-byte-identical {
            statement """
                Flat remains the default and an explicitly-flat request encodes byte-identically to it, so the shipped objective is untouched; the flat encode of test-set/20240501_110934 at 1 bpp still reproduces 97,539 bytes, SSIMULACRA2 62.5738 and butteraugli 5.0325.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-5-donor-implementation-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check donor-scorer-proof {
            statement """
                Tests prove the table the scorer reads equals csf::quant_donor_weights cell for cell on each transform's own grid, that it is mean-1 over the non-LLF cells so lambda stays calibrated, and that the donor curve falls monotonically over its support.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-5-donor-implementation-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check quality-screen {
            statement """
                Matched-rate Butteraugli and SSIMULACRA2 across 7 scenes at 0.5, 1, 2 and 4 bpp, reported split by rate, decide whether the donor weight is promoted under Contract B or recorded as an honest negative; the pre-registered prediction that the 2 bpp arm improves markedly on Phase 6.3's +13.10% mean is reported as confirmed or falsified.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-5-quality-screen-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
    }
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-3-frequency-weight-cover-objective ]
    derived_from [
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.frequency-is-the-live-lever-csf-is-the-wrong-curve-2026-08-12
    ]
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.optimization-correctness-gates
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
    resolves [ @jpegxl-rs.question.which-perceptual-weight-crosses-the-cleanroom-line ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-5b-promote-donor-weight/1 : work {
    title "Phase 6.5b: promote the quantizer-donated frequency weight to the target-rate default"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/lib.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/request.rs",
        path "test-set/**"
    ]
    intent """
        jpegxl-rs.decision.ssimulacra2-is-the-primary-promotion-metric makes SSIMULACRA2 the deciding metric. Phase 6.5's donor weight was rejected under the old rule solely because butteraugli was not uniformly non-regressing; re-read under the new rule it passes clearly and is promoted.

        The evidence is Phase 6.5's own 28-cell screen, unchanged -- no re-measurement is needed because the data was collected on both metrics and only the deciding rule has changed. SSIMULACRA2 improves in 14 of 14 scenes at 0.5 and 1 bpp (mean +4.85 and +0.70, best cell +5.97), is neutral at 2 and 4 bpp (mean -0.0002 and -0.0735, worst single cell -0.18), and pooled improves in 18 of 28 with a mean of +1.37. Butteraugli is also net positive pooled (better in 17 of 28, mean -0.17%), so this is not a metric trade.

        Scope the promotion to EncodeRequest::for_target, matching how Phases 5G and 5I promoted and matching where the screen was measured: every screened encode was target-rate. EncodeRequest::defaults stays the stable fixed-quantizer, restoration-off request, so the Contract A fixed-quantizer fingerprints are untouched.

        The target-rate production fingerprint changes. Record the new one explicitly: the old 97,539 bytes / SSIMULACRA2 62.5738 / butteraugli 5.0325 for test-set/20240501_110934 at 1 bpp has been the baseline sanity check in Phases 6.2b, 6.3 and 6.5 and is cited across those records, so it must be superseded rather than left to rot as a stale assertion.

        No new mechanism ships: CoverFrequencyWeight::QuantDonor already exists, is tested against its published table cell for cell, is mean-1 normalised so lambda stays calibrated, and its curve is derived from the standard's own I.2.5 DCT8x8 matrix rather than fitted to any metric.
        """
    acceptance {
        check corpus-confirms {
            statement """
                A fresh run of the promoted default against the pre-promotion default reproduces Phase 6.5's screened direction on the corpus, confirming the promotion changed what was measured and nothing else.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-5b-promotion-equivalence-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check new-fingerprint-recorded {
            statement """
                The new target-rate production fingerprint for test-set/20240501_110934 at 1 bpp is measured and recorded, explicitly superseding the 97,539 bytes / SSIMULACRA2 62.5738 / butteraugli 5.0325 baseline that Phases 6.2b, 6.3 and 6.5 cite.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-5b-promotion-equivalence-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check target-rate-promoted {
            statement """
                EncodeRequest::for_target selects CoverFrequencyWeight::QuantDonor while EncodeRequest::defaults stays flat, so the fixed-quantizer path remains byte-identical and only the screened target-rate path changes.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase6-5b-promotion-scoped-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
    }
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-5-quant-donor-weight ]
    derived_from [ @jpegxl-rs.observation.donor-weight-wins-at-low-rate-and-fades-2026-08-12 ]
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.ssimulacra2-is-the-primary-promotion-metric
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase7-0-rate-distortion-quantizer/1 : work {
    title "Phase 7.0: make the HF quantizer rate-distortion aware instead of nearest-reconstruction"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-cli/src/main.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/lib.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/quantize.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/request.rs",
        path "test-set/**"
    ]
    intent """
        Three phases now point at the same wall. Phase 6.2b re-priced the cover's transform-size axis and got a null. Phase 6.3 re-priced frequency and got a rate-split. Phase 6.5 re-priced frequency well (median 1.41x against the measured target) and got a large win at 0.5 bpp fading to a no-op at 4 bpp. All three are bounded by the same fact: HfQuantizer::choose picks the nearest reconstruction level, so cover and CfL selection are the only decisions any weight can move, and they matter least exactly where the most coefficients survive quantization.

        sources/outside-advice.md names the same thing directly: quantization is reconstruction-nearest rather than rate-distortion optimized, so it can spend bits on visually unimportant small coefficients while under-protecting important structure elsewhere.

        Make the choice rate-aware. choose already evaluates the candidate set [0, est-1, est, est+1] and picks the smallest |recon - target|; the change is to pick the smallest residual_bits(q) + rd * (recon - target)^2 instead, where rd is exactly the coefficient block_cost_bounded already multiplies squared coefficient error by -- lambda[channel] * side^2 * size_penalty * frequency_weight[cell]. Minimising that per coefficient minimises the same total the cover objective sums, so the quantizer and the cover search finally agree on one currency.

        The rd table is installed on the quantizer at construction rather than threaded through choose's signature, because the decision rule is a property of the operating point. None means nearest, which keeps the shipped path bit-identical.

        KNOWN LIMITATION, stated up front rather than discovered later. residual_bits(q) charges only magnitude bit length plus sign; sources/outside-advice.md is explicit that it knows nothing about entropy context, zero-run behaviour, coefficient order, neighbouring nonzeros, token probabilities or table overhead. The classic rate-distortion quantization win is zeroing a coefficient to extend a zero run, and this proxy cannot see runs at all. So this captures the first-order effect only -- a nonzero coefficient costs at least 2 bits where zero costs none, which widens the effective dead zone where the distortion saved does not pay for the bits -- and a null result would not rule out rate-distortion quantization in general, only this rate proxy.

        Also: choose_lane4 must stay bit-identical to choose (an existing test pins that), and cell_lower_bound's provable prune assumes the nearest rule, so the s8-cover-prune feature is not validated against this mode and must stay off.

        Screen at 0.5/1/2/4 bpp on the same 7-scene corpus and protocol Phases 6.2b/6.3/6.5 used, so the four results are directly comparable. Honest-negative acceptable: if a rate-aware quantizer does not move perceptual quality either, the deficit is not in per-coefficient decisions at all and the track should turn to the entropy model, which is what the rate proxy's crudeness would then be pointing at.
        """
    acceptance {
        check default-byte-identical {
            statement """
                Nearest remains the default and an explicitly-nearest request encodes byte-identically to it; the default encode of test-set/20240501_110934 at 1 bpp still reproduces 97,539 bytes, SSIMULACRA2 62.5738 and butteraugli 5.0325.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-0-rd-implementation-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check quality-screen {
            statement """
                Matched-rate Butteraugli and SSIMULACRA2 across 7 scenes at 0.5, 1, 2 and 4 bpp, reported split by rate and alongside Phases 6.3 and 6.5 on the same protocol, decide whether the rate-aware quantizer is promoted under Contract B or recorded as an honest negative that redirects the track to the entropy model.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-0-quality-screen-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check rd-choice-proof {
            statement """
                Tests prove the rate-distortion mode never selects a candidate whose residual_bits + rd * squared error exceeds another candidate's, that choose_lane4 stays bit-identical to choose under both modes, and that the mode widens the effective dead zone rather than narrowing it.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-0-rd-implementation-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
    }
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase6-5-quant-donor-weight ]
    derived_from [ @jpegxl-rs.observation.donor-weight-wins-at-low-rate-and-fades-2026-08-12 ]
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.optimization-correctness-gates
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase7-1-run-aware-rate-estimate/1 : work {
    title "Phase 7.1: a block-level trailing-nonzero decision priced at its true token saving"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/entropy.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/lib.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/quantize.rs",
        path "test-set/**"
    ]
    intent """
        Phase 7.1a measured the gap and, more usefully, named the lever, which reshapes this work item from what it originally said.

        MEASURED. residual_bits prices only 21.7% / 33.7% / 50.9% of the symbols the encoder actually emits at 0.5 / 1 / 2 bpp -- it is blind to 78.3% / 66.3% / 49.1%. Zero-valued coefficient tokens, real emitted symbols it charges nothing for, are 73.1% / 62.9% / 45.8% of all coefficient tokens.

        THE LEVER IS TRUNCATION, NOT MAGNITUDE. I.4 emits a coefficient token for every order position up to the last nonzero and then stops. So zeroing a block's LAST nonzero frees its own token plus every interior zero back to the previous nonzero -- 1.56 extra tokens on average at 1 bpp -- while zeroing mid-run frees nothing. residual_bits credits a flat ~2 bits for both, and that indifference is the entire Phase 7.0 failure: it zeroed by magnitude alone and stripped texture uniformly, which SSIMULACRA2 penalised in 24 of 28 cells.

        WHAT THAT FORCES. The last-nonzero position is a property of the whole block, so a per-coefficient rule is structurally incapable of this selectivity whatever its lambda -- Phase 7.0 could not have worked as constructed, and no retuning of it would have helped. The design must be a BLOCK-LEVEL BACKWARD PASS: quantize greedily as now, then walk back from the last nonzero considering each trailing nonzero for removal, pricing the drop at its true saving (its own token plus the interior zeros it exposes, plus the reduced non_zeros symbol) against its true weighted distortion, and accepting while rate-distortion favourable. This is the classic last-position decision.

        Scope it as a separate pass over the quantized block rather than a change to choose, so the nearest rule stays the per-coefficient primitive and the new decision is auditable on its own. Keep QuantizerChoiceMode::Nearest as the shipped default until the screen says otherwise.

        PRICING HONESTY. Token counts are exact; token bits are not, because each symbol's cost depends on an ANS cluster the census builds after the walk. Price in tokens with a stated per-token bit estimate, and say so in the record rather than implying the estimate is exact. The neighbour-prediction coupling between blocks (a block's non_zeros feeds later blocks' contexts) is also ignored by a within-block pass and must be recorded as a known approximation.

        SCREEN AND PROMOTION. Re-run the Phase 7.0 screen -- same corpus, same 0.5/1/2/4 bpp, same protocol -- so the two are directly comparable. Under jpegxl-rs.decision.ssimulacra2-is-the-primary-promotion-metric, SSIMULACRA2 decides: the prediction is that a selective rule keeps much of 7.0's butteraugli gain while removing its SSIMULACRA2 loss. If SSIMULACRA2 still regresses, the over-smoothing is not the proxy's fault and the target becomes lambda calibration.

        Report achieved-versus-requested bpp per cell as a first-class number: Phase 7.0's mode worsened the known high-rate undershoot to -7.8% on one cell and confounded two of its own rate rows.
        """
    acceptance {
        check default-byte-identical {
            statement """
                The shipped path remains byte-identical with the pass off.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-1-truncation-proof-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check quality-screen {
            statement """
                The Phase 7.0 screen is re-run on the same corpus, rates and protocol with achieved-versus-requested bpp reported per cell, and SSIMULACRA2 decides promotion under the SSIMULACRA2-primary decision.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-1-quality-screen-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check truncation-saving-proof {
            statement """
                Tests prove the pass prices a trailing-nonzero drop at its true token saving -- its own token plus the interior zeros it exposes plus the reduced non_zeros symbol -- verified against the real walk's emitted symbol count on the same block, not against a reimplementation.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-1-truncation-proof-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
    }
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase7-1a-rate-proxy-gap ]
    derived_from [
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.the-rate-proxy-is-blind-to-most-symbols-and-the-lever-is-truncation-2026-08-12
    ]
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.ssimulacra2-is-the-primary-promotion-metric,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.optimization-correctness-gates
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase7-1a-rate-proxy-gap/1 : work {
    title "Phase 7.1a: measure how much of the rate residual_bits cannot see"
    state completed
    scope [ path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/tests/rate_proxy_gap.rs" ]
    intent """
        Phase 7.0 diagnosed its own SSIMULACRA2 regression as the rate proxy being blind to structure, so it zeroes uniformly instead of selectively. Before building a replacement, size the blindness and identify which specific part of it is the lever -- the same measure-before-design discipline Phases 6.0 and 6.2 used.

        Run the REAL coefficient walk (jpxl_encode::vardct::walk_frame, the function the writer and the census both use, not a reimplementation) over a planned frame and classify every symbol it emits against what residual_bits charges for the same coefficients. Report at several rates, because Phase 6.5 and 7.0 both behaved differently with rate and the proxy's blindness plausibly does too.

        Measurement only: no encoder behaviour changes.
        """
    acceptance {
        check gap-measured {
            statement """
                The share of emitted symbols residual_bits prices at zero is reported at several rates, using the real walk rather than a reimplementation of it.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-1a-gap-measured-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
        check lever-identified {
            statement """
                The record states which specific blindness is the lever and what design that forces, rather than leaving the replacement's shape to be guessed.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-1a-lever-identified-2026-08-12/1 ]
        }
    }
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase7-0-rate-distortion-quantizer ]
    derived_from [
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.the-quantizer-is-the-lever-and-its-rate-proxy-is-the-defect-2026-08-12
    ]
    implements [ @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
}

record jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase7-2-lambda-calibration/1 : work {
    title "Phase 7.2: calibrate lambda, the one term never checked against a perceptual metric"
    state completed
    scope [
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-cli/src/main.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/lib.rs",
        path "JPXL/crates/jpxl-encode-policy/src/request.rs",
        path "test-set/**"
    ]
    intent """
        Four phases now form one argument. Phase 6.2b showed the cover's transform-size axis is inert. Phase 6.5 showed the frequency axis helps and is promoted. Phase 7.0 showed the rate axis moves quality hard -- butteraugli better in 20 of 28 cells -- but against SSIMULACRA2. Phase 7.1 restricted removals to those that actually truncate the I.4 walk, the most rate-efficient removals available anywhere in a block, and still regressed SSIMULACRA2 in 25 of 28 cells while cutting the damage 62%.

        So the problem is not WHICH coefficients get dropped. It is that dropping any HF coefficient at this operating point costs more structure than the bits are worth on SSIMULACRA2's accounting. That is a statement about the exchange rate between rate and distortion, and the exchange rate is lambda.

        lambda is derived in HfQuantizers::new_with_scales as 16 / mean(s^2) over the non-LLF DCT8x8 cells, from a uniform-quantizer argument -- halving a step costs one bit per coefficient and moves s^2/12 to s^2/48. It has never been calibrated against any perceptual metric. If it over-values bits, every rate-aware decision over-zeroes regardless of how selective it is, which is precisely the pattern 7.0 and 7.1 produced.

        Expose a lambda multiplier as a research control and sweep it over the same 28-cell protocol with the Phase 7.1 truncation pass on, since that is the best-behaved rate-aware mode available. Find whether an operating point exists where SSIMULACRA2 is non-regressing and butteraugli keeps its gain.

        Note that ZERO_TOKEN_BITS and lambda are the same knob in effect -- both scale rate against distortion -- so sweeping the former is not an independent experiment and only lambda needs a control.

        Honest-negative pre-registered and genuinely valuable here: if no multiplier gives a non-regressing SSIMULACRA2 while retaining butteraugli gain, then rate-aware quantization is not available to this encoder at ANY exchange rate. That would end this line of enquiry cleanly rather than leaving it as a permanent maybe, and would redirect the track to the entropy model or to the cover, both of which have unexhausted levers.

        Two protocol requirements carried forward. Report achieved-versus-requested bpp per cell: both 7.0 and 7.1 worsened the known high-rate undershoot and partly confounded their own high-rate rows, so the 1 bpp row is the cleanest comparison and should be treated as the primary one. And exclude or separately report s7/0.5, whose flat-baseline SSIMULACRA2 is already negative and which dominated 7.1's 0.5 bpp mean (-0.95 with it, -0.25 without).
        """
    acceptance {
        check default-byte-identical {
            statement """
                The lambda multiplier defaults to 1.0 and the shipped path stays byte-identical.
                """
            method command
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-2-default-byte-identical-2026-08-12 ]
        }
        check operating-point-decided {
            statement """
                The record states explicitly whether an operating point exists where SSIMULACRA2 is non-regressing and butteraugli retains its gain, or that none does and rate-aware quantization is unavailable to this encoder at any exchange rate.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-2-operating-point-decided-2026-08-12 ]
        }
        check sweep-run {
            statement """
                A lambda sweep runs over the 28-cell protocol with the Phase 7.1 truncation pass on, reporting SSIMULACRA2, butteraugli and achieved-versus-requested bpp per cell, with s7/0.5 reported separately.
                """
            method observation
            verified_by [ @jpegxl-rs.evidence.phase7-2-sweep-run-2026-08-12 ]
        }
    }
    depends_on [ @jpegxl-rs.work.arch-phase7-1-run-aware-rate-estimate ]
    derived_from [
        @jpegxl-rs.observation.selectivity-halves-the-damage-but-lambda-is-the-remaining-term-2026-08-12
    ]
    implements [
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.encoder-architecture-phases,
        @jpegxl-rs.decision.ssimulacra2-is-the-primary-promotion-metric,
        @jpegxl-rs.policy.optimization-correctness-gates
    ]
    part_of [ @jpegxl-rs.track.encoder-optimization ]
}

